---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GlobeHero from '../components/GlobeHero.astro';
import CrossfilterDash from '../components/CrossfilterDash.astro';
import FareComparison from '../components/FareComparison.astro';
import AnomalyList from '../components/AnomalyList.astro';
import AnomalyScatter from '../components/AnomalyScatter.astro';
import ForecastChart from '../components/ForecastChart.astro';
import FlightVolumeStreamgraph from '../components/FlightVolumeStreamgraph.astro';
import SlopeChart from '../components/SlopeChart.astro';
import AirlineBreakdown from '../components/AirlineBreakdown.astro';
import summary from '@data/airport_summary.json';

const airports = [
  { code: 'IAD', name: 'Dulles',  color: 'var(--iad)', ...summary.IAD },
  { code: 'DCA', name: 'Reagan',  color: 'var(--dca)', ...summary.DCA },
  { code: 'BWI', name: 'BWI',     color: 'var(--bwi)', ...summary.BWI },
] as const;

function pct(n: number) { return (n * 100).toFixed(1) + '%'; }
function fmt(n: number) { return n.toLocaleString(); }
function fmtk(n: number) { return n >= 1_000_000 ? (n / 1_000_000).toFixed(2) + 'M' : n >= 1_000 ? (n / 1_000).toFixed(0) + 'K' : String(n); }
---

<Layout>
  <GlobeHero />

  <!-- ── Table of contents ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <nav class="toc prose">
    <p class="toc-label">Jump to</p>
    <ol class="toc-list">
      <li><a href="#intro">Overview</a></li>
      <li><a href="#volume">Volume over time</a></li>
      <li><a href="#explore">Explore the data</a></li>
      <li><a href="#fares">Fare comparison</a></li>
      <li><a href="#airlines">Airline breakdown</a></li>
      <li><a href="#delays">When delays happen</a></li>
      <li><a href="#anomalous">Misbehaving routes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#forecast">Forecasts</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limitations">Limitations</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <!-- ── Section 1: Introduction ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>IAD, DCA, BWI — what ten years of data shows</h2>
      <p>
        Growing up in the DMV means you've always got three airports to pick from. IAD out
        in Chantilly, DCA right by the Potomac, and BWI up in Baltimore. I live closest to
        IAD, so it's my default. My flights to Taiwan go through there, though they
        require a layover in Chicago or Japan.
      </p>
      <p>
        One summer I found Tokyo cheaper out of BWI than anything IAD was offering, which
        caught me off guard. I'd always assumed IAD had the best international connections
        and therefore the best international fares. Then there was a Caribbean cruise that
        required me to fly out of Tampa. I ended up on a flight out of IAD for no particular
        reason, just following friends who were already booked on that one. Price drives most of my decisions, but not always, and
        either way I've never actually sat down and checked whether IAD earns its
        convenience premium.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I pulled <strong>3.2 million flights</strong> from the Bureau of Transportation
        Statistics — 2,823,044 domestic and 335,719 international departures — along with
        T-100 capacity data, DB1B fare surveys, and NOAA weather records, all joined
        together. Let's see what the data says.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Stat cards -->
    <div class="cards full-bleed">
      {airports.map(ap => (
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-code" style={`color: ${ap.color}`}>{ap.code}</div>
          <div class="card-name">{ap.name}</div>
          <div class="card-stats">
            <div class="stat">
              <span class="stat-value">{fmt(ap.total_flights + (ap as any).intl_departures)}</span>
              <span class="stat-label">total flights</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat">
              <span class="stat-value">{pct(ap.late_rate)}</span>
              <span class="stat-label">arrived late†</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat">
              <span class="stat-value">{pct(ap.cancel_rate)}</span>
              <span class="stat-label">cancelled†</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="card-intl">+{fmtk((ap as any).intl_departures)} intl departures</div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="cards-footnote full-bleed">† Domestic flights only — BTS on-time data covers domestic routes. "Arrived late" = 15+ min behind scheduled arrival.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 1b: Flight volume streamgraph ─────────────────── -->
  <section id="volume">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Volume over time</h2>
      <p>
        Before anything else, I wanted to see how these airports have actually been doing
        over time. The COVID collapse hit all three in spring 2020, but the severity and
        the recovery looked pretty different for each. DCA took the worst of it, falling to
        just 15% of its 2019 monthly volume by May 2020. IAD dropped to around 30%, BWI
        to about 35%. BWI held up the best during the collapse but ended up
        taking the longest to come back. IAD crossed back to its 2019 baseline by October
        2021, DCA followed a month later, and BWI didn't get there until July 2022. Hover
        over the chart to see the monthly breakdown for each airport.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart full-bleed">
      <FlightVolumeStreamgraph />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 2: Crossfilter ───────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="explore">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Explore 2.7 million domestic flights</h2>
      <p>
        Here's the raw data, all 2.7 million domestic departures. You can filter by airport,
        drag across any of the charts to select a range (time of day, month, delay, distance)
        and everything else updates to match. It's a good way to poke around and see if anything
        jumps out at you.
      </p>
      <p>
        The count is 2.7 million rather than 3.2 million because the 335,719 international
        flights are excluded (BTS on-time data is domestic only), and the 75,040 cancelled
        flights are excluded since they have no arrival delay. What's left is every domestic
        flight that actually took off and landed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart full-bleed">
      <CrossfilterDash />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 3: Fare comparison ──────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="fares">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Does it actually cost more to fly out of IAD?</h2>
      <p>
        This is the question I actually wanted to answer. There are 52 domestic destinations
        that all three airports fly to with enough volume to get a reliable average fare. The
        trend chart shows how overall fares moved year by year. The dot plot puts all three
        airports side by side for each route — further left means cheaper.
      </p>
      <p>
        Short answer: yes, IAD is more expensive, and it's not subtle. BWI is the cheapest
        option on 44 of those 52 routes, and IAD is the priciest on 22. If you're driving
        past DCA or BWI to get to Dulles, you're probably paying for that choice.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart full-bleed">
      <FareComparison />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 4b: Airline breakdown ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="airlines">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Who's flying out of each airport</h2>
      <p>
        The fare gap between airports is real, but it's not really about the airports themselves.
        Carrier mix explains most of it. United controls 68% of IAD's traffic. Southwest runs 60% of BWI.
        Those two carriers don't charge the same fares. When you look at overall medians, the differences
        are small: Southwest runs $188–198 across all three airports, United $250–267. But look at specific routes both carriers fly from IAD and BWI and the picture shifts.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart full-bleed">
      <AirlineBreakdown />
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:3rem;margin-bottom:3rem">
      <p>
        Southwest is actually cheaper from IAD than from BWI on 40 of 43 shared routes.
        IAD to Orlando is $108 with Southwest. BWI to Orlando, same carrier, is $182. That holds
        across most shared routes, with a median gap of $23 cheaper at IAD. BWI is Southwest's
        hub, where they control 60% of the traffic and face less competition on most routes. At IAD
        they're a 6% player, so they price accordingly.
      </p>
      <p>
        United is the opposite. On the 73 routes where United flies from both airports, IAD runs
        about $23 more expensive than BWI, and that direction holds on 74% of routes. IAD is United's
        hub, so the same logic applies in reverse.
      </p>
      <p>
        The $40 median gap between IAD and BWI is a few things stacked on top of each other.
        Carrier mix is the bigger part: a 68% United airport is always going to average higher than
        a 60% Southwest airport. But the hub premium is in there too. Both carriers charge more where
        they dominate, and they happen to dominate opposite ends of the price spectrum.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 4: Slope chart ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="delays">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>When delays happen</h2>
      <p>
        Morning flights are almost always more reliable than evening flights — delays
        accumulate throughout the day. But how much worse does it actually get, and does
        that gap change by season? Each panel here compares the morning late rate (6–10am)
        against the evening late rate (6–10pm). Hover over a panel to see the exact numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Summer is the worst by a wide margin. All three airports nearly double their late
        rate from morning to evening. Fall is the most forgiving season if you have to book
        an evening flight and can't afford to gamble.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart full-bleed">
      <SlopeChart />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 4: Anomalous routes ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="anomalous">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Routes that keep misbehaving</h2>
      <p>
        Not all delays are equal. Some routes run late on a bad day; others have been structurally
        broken for years. To separate the two, I looked at 174 routes with enough history to establish
        a pre-pandemic baseline, requiring at least three years of data from 2015–2019 and at least 100
        flights a year. For each route, I built a picture of what "normal" looked like using four metrics:
        what fraction of flights arrived late, the average delay in minutes, how much of that delay
        came from the air-traffic control system rather than the carrier, and the cancellation rate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I checked whether 2022 and beyond fit that picture. The approach works by asking how easy
        it is to statistically isolate a data point from everything else. A route-year that blends into
        the crowd is normal; one that stands apart is an outlier. Crucially, each route is judged against
        its own history, not against other routes. That controls for the fact that a cross-country
        red-eye is going to have different base delay rates than a regional hop.
      </p>
      <p>
        Showing all four features at once requires a different kind of chart. A scatter plot can
        only put two dimensions on its axes, which means a route anomalous primarily because its
        cancellation rate spiked would look completely normal on a late-arrival versus delay plot.
        A parallel coordinates chart solves this by giving each feature its own vertical axis and
        drawing each route as a line that passes through all four. The gray background is every
        normal route-year observation, showing the shape of typical performance. Each colored line
        is one of the 75 persistent routes, drawn as the average of its anomalous years, so you
        see its overall deviation profile rather than year-to-year noise. The shape of a line
        shows where the deviation is: a line that runs high on the first two axes but flat on the
        last two is a delay problem; one that spikes on cancellation rate tells a different story.
        Hover any colored line to see the route and its averaged values.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart prose">
      <AnomalyScatter />
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A single bad year could be weather, a labor dispute, a one-off event. To find routes with
        a genuine structural problem, I kept only those that stood out in at least two of the five
        post-2022 years. That comes to 75 out of 174: 36 at BWI, 26 at DCA, and 13 at IAD. Nine
        were flagged in all five years without exception: BWI→ATL, BWI→MDW, BWI→CLT, BWI→SAT,
        DCA→CLT, DCA→STL, IAD→SFO, IAD→TPA, and IAD→SLC. That the share is this high reflects
        how broadly performance shifted after the pandemic. These routes were not just having an
        off year; they deviated from their own pre-pandemic baseline in multiple dimensions,
        repeatedly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bars below show persistence across all 75 routes. A full bar means the route stood out
        in its delay profile every year since 2022 and has not had a clean stretch.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart prose">
      <AnomalyList />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 5: Forecasts ──────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="forecast">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What the models say</h2>
      <p>
        Prophet models were fitted on monthly late rates from 2015–2021 and
        projected through 2026. The dashed line is the forecast; solid lines are actuals.
        The shaded band is the 95% confidence interval.
      </p>
      <p>
        IAD and DCA are tracking close to forecast. BWI is the outlier: it spiked
        well above its expected trend starting in 2022 and has only partially recovered,
        averaging +4 percentage points above forecast through 2024.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-chart prose">
      <ForecastChart />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Section 6: Limitations ────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="limitations">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No international delay data — BTS on-time performance covers domestic flights only. International route arcs on the globe show passenger volume, not delays.</li>
        <li>No international fare data — DB1B is domestic only by design.</li>
        <li>NOAA weather coverage ends August 2025; 4.5% of rows have no weather features.</li>
        <li>DB1B is a 10% ticket sample — fare figures are estimates.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
<script>
  const TOOLTIP_IDS = ['fare-tooltip', 'fvs-tt', 'slope-tt', 'ab-tt'];

  function hideAll() {
    for (const id of TOOLTIP_IDS) {
      const el = document.getElementById(id);
      if (el) el.style.opacity = '0';
    }
  }

  window.addEventListener('scroll', hideAll, { passive: true });

  document.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
    const t = e.target as Element;
    if (!t.closest('svg') && !t.closest('.slope-panel')) hideAll();
  });
</script>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Table of contents */
  .toc {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .toc-label {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .toc-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.35rem 0.75rem;
  }
  .toc-list li {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .toc-list a {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: #555;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #2a2d3a;
    padding-bottom: 1px;
    transition: color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .toc-list a:hover {
    color: #aaa;
    border-color: #555;
  }

  /* Stat cards */
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 0 1.5rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      padding: 0 1.25rem;
      margin-top: 1.75rem;
    }
    .card { padding: 1.25rem; }
    .card-code { font-size: 1.5rem; }
  }
  .card {
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid #2a2d3a;
  }
  .card-code {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .card-name {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    margin: 0.25rem 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .card-stats {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }
  .card-intl {
    margin-top: 0.65rem;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    border-top: 1px solid #2a2d3a;
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .stat {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.125rem;
  }
  .stat-value {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #fff;
  }
  .stat-label {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .section-chart {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .cards-footnote {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0 1.5rem;
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  ul li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
</style>
